  1875	
  1876	Owner direction (2026-10-12): a module published by another principal is
  1877	not refused automatically. Which principals a session trusts is a
  1878	configurable policy, and the user of a module may choose to trust its
  1879	principal. The policy is an interpreter of a stream, the shape ShiBi
  1880	already has (`dao.shibi.md`).
  1881	
  1882	#### 7.5.1 The datums
  1883	
  1884	When the DHT link source resolves a name, it appends an admission
  1885	question to an **admission request medium**. One interpreter reads the
  1886	questions and appends answers to an **admission response medium**. The
  1887	two ring media sit beside the link pair. The admission id is
  1888	`[link-id serial]`. `serial` is a per-composition counter, never reused,
  1889	so a retired question and a later question for the same link id never
  1890	share an id.
  1891	
  1892	```clojure
  1893	;; question, appended to the admission request medium at resolution
  1894	{:yin.admit/id         [[:t0 7] 3]
  1895	 :yin.link/id          [:t0 7]
  1896	 :yin.admit/name       'pym.m
  1897	 :yin.admit/address    :segment/blake3-...   ; the resolved manifest address, retained
  1898	 :yin.admit/asserters  ["ed25519:<hex>" ...] ; every asserter of that entry (R8)
  1899	 :yin.admit/provenance {...}                 ; the entry's :yin.link/provenance verbatim
  1900	 :yin.link/format      f                     ; the request's own words, no subject
  1901	 :yin.link/contract    "b2"}
  1902	
  1903	;; answers, appended to the admission response medium
  1904	{:yin.admit/id [[:t0 7] 3] :yin.admit/decision :allow  :yin.admit/conditions {}}
  1905	{:yin.admit/id [[:t0 7] 3] :yin.admit/decision :refuse
  1906	 :yin.admit/reason :yin.admit/untrusted-principal :yin.admit/asserters [...]}
  1907	```
  1908	
  1909	- The question's key set is closed: exactly the keys above. No session
  1910	  and no subject travels in it; the subject is the interpreter's context
  1911	  (7.5.6). No key of the question or of the link request is
  1912	  `:yin.admit/subject`, `:yin.admit/session`, or in the `dao.shibi`
  1913	  namespace.
  1914	- The allow answer's key set is closed: `:yin.admit/id`,
  1915	  `:yin.admit/decision :allow` and `:yin.admit/conditions`. A refuse
  1916	  answer carries `:yin.admit/id`, `:yin.admit/decision :refuse` and
  1917	  `:yin.admit/reason`, and may carry further keys, which are copied into
  1918	  the link refusal.
  1919	- A direct `:name-env` entry (provenance `:composition`) is not asked.
  1920	  A non-DHT link source has no admission pair.
  1921	- The link response on allow gains `:yin.admit/id`,
  1922	  `:yin.admit/decision :allow`, `:yin.admit/asserters` and
  1923	  `:yin.admit/address`. A refusal copies into the link refusal shape with
  1924	  the answer's reason and its open keys.
  1925	
  1926	#### 7.5.2 Ask after resolve, before load: the retained entry (F1)
  1927	
  1928	`link/serve` takes `{:pair :source :dht :admission}` and returns
  1929	`{:pair :pending :progress? :dht :admission}`. The admission state is a
  1930	value:
  1931	
  1932	```clojure
  1933	{:pair    {:questions q-medium :answers a-medium :cursor c}  ; c: the composition's answer cursor
  1934	 :asked   {[:t0 7] question}        ; the retained question, at most one per pair in v1
  1935	 :answers {[[:t0 7] 3] answer}      ; drained, keyed by admission id, first answer wins
  1936	 :serial  4}
  1937	```
  1938	
  1939	The question is asked after the name resolves and before any load
  1940	starts. The DHT branch of the link answer takes three cases, in this
  1941	order:
  1942	
  1943	1. **Asked and unanswered.** `:asked` holds an entry for the link id and
  1944	   `:answers` holds nothing under that entry's admission id: the link is
  1945	   pending, and the pending entry carries `:admission <id>` beside
  1946	   `:name` and `:yin.link/id`. The name is not resolved again.
  1947	2. **Asked and answered.** An allow loads the **retained** address, the
  1948	   `:yin.admit/address` of the asked question, never a fresh
  1949	   `resolve-name`. A refuse is the link refusal. When the link response
  1950	   lands, the asked entry and its answer are dropped.
  1951	3. **Not asked.** The name resolves as before. An `:ok` resolution mints
  1952	   the question with the next serial, appends it to the request medium,
  1953	   records it under `:asked`, and reports the link pending with
  1954	   `:admission`. A question append counts as progress. A refused append
  1955	   leaves nothing asked and reports no progress.
  1956	
  1957	**A question-bound link is never refreshed.** Serve stops at the first
  1958	pending request, so at most one question is open per pair, bound to the
  1959	entry it was minted from until an answer lands or the request is retired.
  1960	A head change after the question is asked does not move the address the
  1961	allow loads. If the retained address fails to load after an allow, the
  1962	link is refused as any failed load is, and the asked entry goes with it.
  1963	The user requires again; the new link id gets a new question over the
  1964	head as it then is.
  1965	
  1966	**The registry record.** `engine/link-install` assocs onto the installed
  1967	registry entry, after the module entry is built and before the module is
  1968	received, an `:admission` map `{:yin.admit/id :yin.admit/decision
  1969	:yin.admit/asserters :yin.admit/address}` copied from a response that
  1970	carries `:yin.admit/decision :allow`. Install children inherit entries
  1971	whole and parents receive transferred entries whole, so the record is
  1972	inherited and transferred with them. A direct `:name-env` entry carries
  1973	no `:admission` key.
  1974	
  1975	#### 7.5.3 Parking, with no deadline
  1976	
  1977	A link whose question is unanswered parks. Nothing times it out: there is
  1978	no clock in admission, and liveness belongs to `dao.lease` and to the
  1979	`:link-policy` (8.3). An unanswered question ends only when an answer
  1980	lands or its request is retired by abandonment.
  1981	
  1982	**Abandonment retires the run.** `mint-link-id` emits the current
  1983	counter and then increments it, and child origins are minted the same way
  1984	(origin `:t0.<n>` with `n` the current `:origins`). Both ranges are
  1985	therefore half-open. `(abandon)` computes a run descriptor
  1986	`{:origin :t0 :ids [from until] :children [from until]}` from `base`, the
  1987	parked run's `(:base parked)`, and `parked`, the VM after the abandoned
  1988	installs and links are folded out, with every counter normalized by
  1989	`(or counter 0)`:
  1990	
  1991	- `:ids` is `[(:id-counter base) (:id-counter parked)]`.
  1992	- `:children` is `[(:origins base) (:origins parked)]`.
  1993	
  1994	`(link/retire {:pair :admission} run)` returns
  1995	`{:pair :admission :retired [ids]}`. Walking the link pair's requests with
  1996	`stream/next` from the pair's request cursor, a request with id `[o k]` is
  1997	retired iff:
  1998	
  1999	- `o` is the run's origin and `base <= k < parked-counter`, that is
  2000	  `from <= k < until` of `:ids`; or
  2001	- for some `m` with `from <= m < until` of `:children`, with
  2002	  `child = (str (name origin) "." m)`, `(name o)` equals `child` or starts
  2003	  with `child + "."`, the exact origin or `origin + "."` of that child.
  2004	
  2005	A raw prefix never matches: `"t0.10"` does not start with `"t0.1."`, so
  2006	`:t0.10` is never a descendant of `:t0.1`. Nested install children
  2007	(`:t0.4.0`, `:t0.4.0.2`) match through the `"t0.4."` prefix. Each retired
  2008	request advances the cursor. The first request that is not retired stops
  2009	the walk, keeping serve's stop-at-first-unanswered rule, so a request
  2010	outside the run is never skipped. Retire removes every `:asked` entry
  2011	whose link id was retired and every `:answers` entry under those ids. No
  2012	link response is appended for a retired request: the engine has already
  2013	retired the id, and a response would be `:late`. An answer arriving later
  2014	for a retired id correlates to nothing and the drain drops it.
  2015	
  2016	`:retired` names the run-owned requests the walk still encounters from the
  2017	current cursor, before the first unrelated request. A root request that
  2018	was already answered and spawned an install child is behind the cursor and
  2019	is not in `:retired`; its child's requests are.
  2020	
  2021	#### 7.5.4 The drain: bounded, consuming, with a persisted cursor
  2022	
  2023	One public `(admission/drain admission budget)` returns the successor
  2024	admission state. It reads the response medium with `stream/next` from
  2025	`:pair :cursor`, at most `budget` elements, and persists the cursor past
  2026	everything it read. Every read consumes; nothing peeks.
  2027	
  2028	- **Admission id comparison is keyed by `[link-id serial]`.** `:asked` is
  2029	  keyed by link id, and an answer by its full admission id. An answer `a`
  2030	  is kept only when its id equals the retained question's id for the
  2031	  answer's link id:
  2032	
  2033	  ```clojure
  2034	  (= (:yin.admit/id a)
  2035	     (:yin.admit/id (get asked (first (:yin.admit/id a)))))
  2036	  ```
  2037	
  2038	  and `:answers` holds nothing under that id yet. An answer with a stale
  2039	  serial for a live link id, a duplicate (the first answer wins), and an
  2040	  answer for an unknown or retired id are consumed and dropped.
  2041	- On `:dao.stream/gap` the drain adopts the recovery cursor the transport
  2042	  returns (the earliest retained position, never an evicted one) and
  2043	  continues within the budget. On `:dao.stream/blocked` or
  2044	  `:dao.stream/end` it stops.
  2045	- Serve calls `drain` with `serve-budget` (64) at the start of each round.
  2046	  The host hook (7.5.5) calls the same function.
  2047	- The media have capacity `pair-capacity` (1024) and evict the oldest
  2048	  element. An answer is lost when the unread backlog on the response
  2049	  medium exceeds that capacity; host composition must bound the
  2050	  accumulated backlog through its production rate and its drain cadence.
  2051	  A lost answer leaves its question pending until retirement. There is no
  2052	  re-answer protocol.
  2053	
  2054	#### 7.5.5 Late answers and host driving
  2055	
  2056	All without a clock.
  2057	
  2058	- **In the drive.** `drive-links` runs `admission/serve` right after
  2059	  `link/serve` and assocs its admission successor on the state before the
  2060	  VM runs, so a raising run carries it (below). A round continues while
  2061	  the link serve, the admission serve or the answer drain made progress.
  2062	  With the in-process interpreter one require costs two extra rounds.
  2063	  With none, the drive ends with the link pending, bounded by
  2064	  `link-round-budget` as before.
  2065	- **The host hook.** `(yin.repl/recheck-on-answers state)` is a sibling of
  2066	  `recheck-on-load-events`, called beside it by `yin.repl.main/step-all`
  2067	  (8.2). It drains with `serve-budget`, persists the successor on the
  2068	  state whether or not anything matched, and calls `recheck-pending` once
  2069	  iff a full key of the retained `:answers` map equals the `:admission` id
  2070	  of a pending entry of the pending run. Otherwise it answers
  2071	  `[state' nil]` with no `:checks` change. Irrelevant answers advance the
  2072	  cursor and are gone; a valid answer behind `serve-budget` irrelevant
  2073	  ones is drained on the next tick. With no pending run the hook still
  2074	  drains.
  2075	- **The command path.** `(trust p)` rebuilds the session decision and,
  2076	  when a run is pending, calls `recheck-pending` once.
  2077	- **Error rollback.** The link raise carries the admission successor beside
  2078	  the link pair, and `carry-served` restores it, so after an install that
  2079	  raises the asked entry is dropped, the answer consumed, and the cursor
  2080	  and serial advanced. `(abandon)` uses `retire` on the live state instead.
  2081	
  2082	#### 7.5.6 The interpreter contract
  2083	
  2084	`yin.repl.admission` is composed from
  2085	`{:trusted #{...} :verify f :decision d :decision-medium m}` and answers
  2086	each question with `(verify decision context question)`: nil allows, a
  2087	reason map refuses. The context is composition-owned, built by
  2088	`admission/context` from the session:
  2089	`{:yin.admit/subject "ed25519:<hex>" | nil, :yin.admit/session <token>}`,
  2090	the session token being the shell token now and `[id revision]` once the
  2091	REPL F2 slice lands. Authorization vocabulary stays in the context and the
  2092	policy; the question and the linker carry none.
  2093	
  2094	- **Composition-bounded synchronous verification.** `verify` runs
  2095	  synchronously inside one serve round on one snapshot. Its cost per
  2096	  question is bounded by the composition, and the interpreter answers at
  2097	  most `serve-budget` questions per round. `verify` opens, fetches and
  2098	  waits for nothing; a rule that would need a remote read is a rule the
  2099	  decision snapshot must already hold (`dao.shibi.md`, limits of the
  2100	  adapter contract).
  2101	- **The snapshot.** The decision snapshot is the static `:decision`
  2102	  value, or the newest value on a capacity-1 `:decision-medium`, read once
  2103	  per round. An **absent** snapshot gives no answer: the question stays
  2104	  pending and the round reports no progress. An **ended** snapshot
  2105	  refuses `:yin.admit/decision-ended`, with no load started.
  2106	- **The default interpreter.** The decision is the session's own
  2107	  principal together with the `:trusted` set. It allows when some
  2108	  asserter of the question is in that set, and otherwise refuses
  2109	  `:yin.admit/untrusted-principal` naming the asserters.
  2110	- **Composition.** Policies compose as verify functions; the first reason
  2111	  wins. One interpreter writes the response medium.
  2112	- **At the REPL.** `(trust p)` adds principal `p` to the session's trusted
  2113	  set, `(untrust p)` removes it, and `(trusted)` prints the own principal
  2114	  and the trusted set. `create-state` takes `:admission` beside
  2115	  `:link-policy`, defaulting to the own key from `:dht-key`. `(reset)`
  2116	  drops the trusted set unless it was given in `create-state`.
  2117	
  2118	This is not the gate seam of `dao.shibi.md`. It reuses the `verify`
  2119	signature and the snapshot, but a request read off a stream and answered
  2120	with one decision per request is the path `dao.shibi.md` names as a
  2121	non-goal for the gate, to be added beside `verify`. This section is that
  2122	addition. A ShiBi interpreter later replaces or composes with the default
  2123	one as another verify function over its own published snapshot, with no
  2124	edit to the linker.
  2125	
  2126	#### 7.5.7 Exposure: the session's registry
  2127	
  2128	Admission governs entry into a session's module registry, and
  2129	authorization follows the session and the registry it inherits. A cache
  2130	hit answers a require from the registry without a question; install
  2131	children inherit registry entries; parents receive what children
  2132	installed; `(lang ...)` preserves the registry. One allow therefore
  2133	authorizes every later require of that module in that session.
  2134	
  2135	- `(untrust p)` is not retroactive: it changes only later answers, and a
  2136	  module already in the registry stays usable.
  2137	- `(reset)` and `(vm ...)` start a fresh registry and a fresh admission
  2138	  state.
  2139	
  2140	#### 7.5.8 Trust versus safety
  2141	
  2142	Trust is which principals a session accepts, and it is the admission
  2143	interpreter's answer. Safety is the linker's and L-e's, enforced whatever
  2144	the answer says: closure origin, store ownership, dependency pins and the
  2145	primitives profile (`yang.antlr.md` 8.5.6, ruling 19). **An allow never
  2146	relaxes L-e.** The interpreter sees names, addresses, asserters and
  2147	provenance, never bytes.
  2148	
  2149	**Conditions are reserved empty in v1.** `:yin.admit/conditions` must be
  2150	`{}`. A primitive or effect bound on a trusted module is future work and
  2151	does not land before L-e.
  2152	
  2153	#### 7.5.9 Fail closed
  2154	
  2155	Every reason below is final for that link:
  2156	
  2157	- A DHT link source with a nil `:admission` refuses
  2158	  `:yin.admit/unconfigured`.
  2159	- A malformed answer refuses `:yin.admit/malformed-answer`.
  2160	- An answer with non-empty conditions refuses
  2161	  `:yin.admit/unsupported-condition`.
  2162	- An ended decision snapshot refuses `:yin.admit/decision-ended`.
  2163	- The default interpreter refuses an untrusted asserter set
  2164	  `:yin.admit/untrusted-principal`.
  2165	
  2166	An absent snapshot and an unanswered question never allow; they leave the
  2167	link pending until an answer or retirement.
  2168	
  2169	**Must not land before L-e:** anything that claims untrusted-origin
  2170	imports are safe; a default decision other than own principals; honoring
  2171	any non-empty condition; a weakening of profile or dependency-pin
  2172	discharge or of export-origin validation; admission on the content pair
  2173	or in any frontend. Everything here is AST-level data on streams, so the
  2174	register-VM cutover does not affect it.
  2175	
